\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{The canonical topology on local points}

\p{Here a local field #{K} of characteristic different from #{2} means
#{\mathbb R} or #{\mathbb C} with its usual topology, or a complete
discretely valued field with finite residue field and #{\operatorname{char}K\ne2},
with its valuation topology. The last case includes finite extensions of
#{\mathbb Q_2}: the characteristic restriction is on #{K}, not its residue
field. This uses the nonarchimedean definition in
\citet{VI.2, Definition 2.1 and the discussion, pp. 151--152}{lam2005quadratic},
with the two archimedean fields included explicitly.}

\p{For a finite-dimensional #{K}-vector space #{W}, its canonical topology
is the topology transported from #{K^d} by any basis, where
#{d=\dim_K W}. It is independent of the basis: a change of coordinates
and its inverse have entries that are finite linear combinations, hence
are continuous. In dimension zero this gives the one-point topology.
Every linear map between such spaces is continuous by the same argument.}

\p{More generally, the canonical topology on the local points of an
affine algebraic set is the subspace topology in any finite coordinate
presentation. Explicitly, let #{X(K)} consist of the points #{x\in K^m}
satisfying the finite polynomial equations

##{\begin{gathered}
f_i(x)=0\\
(i=1,\ldots,r).
\end{gathered}}

Give #{X(K)} the topology inherited from #{K^m}. Changing the finite
algebraic presentation expresses each new coordinate as a polynomial in
the old ones and conversely. Both coordinate maps are continuous, so this
topology is independent of the presentation. This is the concrete affine
construction in \citet{Proposition 2.1 and its proof, p. 2}{conrad2011adelic}.
It also shows directly that polynomial maps on local points are continuous.}

\p{Let #{A} be a finite-dimensional unital #{K}-algebra, with its canonical
vector-space topology. Its multiplication is continuous because its
coordinates are bilinear polynomials. On the group of units #{A^\times},
the affine-point construction first gives the inverse-graph topology:

##{\begin{gathered}
A^\times\longrightarrow A\times A,\\
x\longmapsto(x,x^{-1}).
\end{gathered}}

This identifies the units with the solution set of #{xy=yx=1}.
The inverse coordinate matters in the general topological-ring
construction; see \citet{opening of Section 3, pp. 3--4}{conrad2011adelic}.}

\p{Over the local fields specified above, this is also the subspace
topology from #{A}. Indeed, write #{L_x} for the matrix of left
multiplication by #{x}. Its entries depend linearly on #{x}, and for a
unit #{x},

##{\begin{gathered}
x^{-1}=L_x^{-1}(1),\\
L_x^{-1}=\frac{\operatorname{adj}(L_x)}{\det L_x}.
\end{gathered}}

Polynomial continuity and continuity of reciprocal on #{K^\times} show
that inversion is continuous on the subspace #{A^\times\subseteq A}.
Thus the inverse-graph map is continuous for that subspace topology;
projection to the first coordinate is its continuous inverse.
For the zero algebra the unit group is a singleton and the same topology
agreement is immediate.}

\p{For a quadratic form #{Q} on a finite-dimensional #{V}, apply this
construction to #{A=\Cl(Q)} and to #{\operatorname{End}_K(V)}.
The orthogonal and special orthogonal groups inherit the matrix topology
from #{\operatorname{End}_K(V)}. The actual vector-generated Lipschitz,
Pin, and Spin carriers inherit the subspace topology from
#{\Cl(Q)^\times}, equivalently from #{\Cl(Q)}. These topologies depend
on the field topology and the algebraic carriers, without a choice of
positive-definite norm or a replacement of the actual carriers by
normalizer-defined groups.}

\p{The coordinate topology on #{W} is also its module topology: it is
the finest topology making addition and scalar multiplication continuous.
The coordinate topology has these properties; for any other such topology,
the basis map #{K^d\to W}, a finite sum of scalar multiples of fixed basis
vectors, is continuous. This proves the claimed maximality.
Mathlib's \leanref{moduleTopology} defines that intrinsic topology, and
TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.instTopologicalSpaceCliffordAlgebra}
uses it for #{\Cl(Q)}. The agreement with local coordinates follows from
the finite-dimensional argument above; the declaration itself is defined
over a general topological commutative ring.}
